The hyperbola shown has the form $y=\dfrac{k}{x}$ and passes through $(2,-1)$. Find $k$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5, xmax=5, ymin=-5, ymax=5, samples=200, clip=false, restrict y to domain=-6:6, unbounded coords=jump, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,-2,2,4}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4}]
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=0.4:5] {-2/x};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=-5:-0.4] {-2/x};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(2,-1)};
\node[below right] at (axis cs:2,-1) {$(2,-1)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The hyperbola shown has vertical asymptote $x=-2$ and horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Which equation matches it?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5, xmax=5, ymin=-5, ymax=5, samples=200, clip=false, restrict y to domain=-6:6, unbounded coords=jump, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,-2,2,4}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4}]
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.8:5] {1/(x+2)};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=-5:-2.2] {1/(x+2)};
\addplot[dashed, red] coordinates {(-2,-5) (-2,5)};
\node[above left] at (axis cs:-2,4.2) {$x=-2$};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(-1,1)};
\node[above right] at (axis cs:-1,1) {$(-1,1)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
